# TEMPORARY, together with the ci-testing entry in build-on-push.yml.
#
# One "<kernel> <url> [<suites>] [<variant>]" per line. Each named kernel is
# tested against the build at that URL and nothing is rebuilt. The kernel has to
# be one of the keys of .github/lava-boards.yml, which is what decides the
# boards:
#
#   qcom-next   https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/35226840721-1/
#   linux-next  https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/35100000000-1/
#   qcom-7.3    https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/35300000000-1/ trixie xfce
#
# <suites> is a comma-separated list of the suites to test, trixie and forky
# when left out; name it for a build that has images for only some of them.
# <variant> is the image variant to test (e.g. xfce), the console images when
# left out; giving one means giving <suites> as well.
#
# Leave it with no entry and the qcom-next images are built and tested as they
# are on main.
#
# The builds below are the latest of each daily workflow whose debos jobs all
# succeeded, as of 2026-11-20. Several of those runs are still red: it is their
# LAVA half that failed, not the images these URLs point at.
qcom-next   https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/44615698834-1/
arduino     https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/44650552042-1/
linux-next  https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/44678662967-1/
debian      https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/44692446790-1/
mainline    https://qli-prod-artifacts.qualcomm.com/qcom-prd-gh-artifacts/qualcomm-linux/qcom-deb-images/44493220277-1/
#
# A URL is the artifact prefix of a previous run, which holds the images of
# every suite. Take it from the "Downloads URL" line of that run's debos job, or
# from the build-url metadata of any LAVA job it submitted.
#
# Why one URL per kernel rather than a kernel axis on the build job: debos
# uploads to <run id>-<attempt>/ and names the images after the suite alone, so
# two kernels built in one run would write the same file over each other. Each
# kernel gets its own build only by being its own workflow run, which on main is
# what the daily workflows are.
